Page-table physical backing and fragment retention
==================================================

These bounded fixtures reserve 2 GiB of virtual address space and write one
4 KiB leaf in each of 1,024 distinct 2 MiB table windows. Native payload
backing is at most 16 MiB, with approximately 4 MiB of logical PTE tables.
The concurrent variant uses 32 threads, 32 synchronized fault rounds and
128 KiB thread stacks. Both verify all payload bytes across six checkpoints:
baseline, full, quarter_survivors, refilled, quarter_survivors_again, unmapped.

Run through the 4 KiB ABI launcher on a native 16 KiB kernel. Each JSON
checkpoint requires one byte 'c' on stdin before continuing. For contents
only, with no physical charge observation:

  yes c | userpages 4k ./pagetable-retention --native-16k
  yes c | userpages 4k ./pagetable-concurrent --native-16k

For physical accounting, run each fixture alone in a fresh uncapped cgroup.
At each checkpoint verify the fixture is the only member, record memory.stat
pagetables and memory.swap.current, and sample at 0, 1 and 3 seconds before
acknowledging. Record logical VmPTE separately: it is not physical backing.
Use an external service lifetime below the fixture's 60-second alarm and
verify all processes exit. Do not change memory.max/high or force reclaim.

Compare the same complete fixture across kernels. Concurrent thread stacks
and upper-level tables contribute to the measurements; do not subtract them
as though they were pure payload. Deferred table releases can occur between
samples. Contents PASS does not assert a density or performance result.

The allocation-race fix reduces initial concurrent physical backing. It does
not reclaim partially occupied table owners or reuse issued fragments after
unmap. Retention/refill checkpoints deliberately preserve that separate
coverage gap rather than declaring it fixed.
